WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.190
With acclaimed roles in Deliverance and Smokey
and the Bandit, Burt Reynolds's swagger and

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.210
charm made him a huge star in the '70s.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.370
But despite his box-office clout and sex symbol
status, the once-great star seems to have

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.020
faded away.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.000
So, why aren't we seeing more from Burt Reynolds?

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.660
Becoming a star

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.130
Reynolds began his career in the late '50s
and worked steadily in small TV roles through

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:23.320
the '60s, including an episode of The Twilight
Zone, playing a cocky actor who gets punched

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.340
in the face by a time-traveling Shakespeare.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.680
“What have I got against this personage,
Stanislavsky?

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:31.920
You!”

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.140
But in 1972, Deliverance gave Reynolds his
first major movie role.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.650
However, just before the film was released,
Reynolds was invited to pose nude for Cosmopolitan.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.530
It was the first nude male centerfold in a
major magazine, and he figured people would

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.860
look at and laugh and forget about it a few
days later.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.690
But instead of being a laugh riot, it made
Reynolds the sex symbol of the '70s.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.480
It also diminished his serious acting accomplishments
in Deliverance.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:04.500
“I was very young and very stupid, and it
did not change my career to the better.”

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.080
Reynolds had become a huge star, but he was
never really taken seriously, which would

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.540
haunt him for the rest of his career.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.140
A few bad choices

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.500
Reynolds' signature 'stache and cocky smile
won over male and female fans alike through

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:19.649
the '70s, but by the '80s, his movies were
already getting less critical acclaim.

00:01:19.649 --> 00:01:24.069
1981's The Cannonball Run featured an all-star
cast and made a lot of money — but critics

00:01:24.069 --> 00:01:24.720
weren't pleased.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.220
Also released in 1981 was the well-reviewed
drama Sharky's Machine, which had Reynolds

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:29.960
as its star.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.680
But he squandered those critical gains by
following that up with Best Little Whorehouse

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.140
in Texas, the forgettable Best Friends, and
the horribly reviewed Stroker Ace.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.909
By the time 1984 rolled around, Reynolds returned
for Cannonball Run II, which got even worse

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:46.200
reviews and made $44 million less than the
original.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.749
By sticking to a run of mediocre comedies,
Reynolds wasn't being taken seriously, and

00:01:49.749 --> 00:01:52.000
he could only charm audiences for so long.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.409
And if the first half of the '80s seemed troublesome
for Reynolds, the second half only got worse.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:01.049
Reynolds' work included a cameo in the bizarre
Smokey and the Bandit Part 3 and the terribly-reviewed

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:01.549
Rent-A-Cop.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.280
Things didn't get any better from there.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.899
Too many refusals

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.840
Over the years, Reynolds turned down a lot
of roles that would wind up becoming iconic.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.310
In an interview with Business Insider, Reynolds
revealed that he could've played the first

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.690
American James Bond, but he felt 007 was a
role only an Englishman could play.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:21.000
And before Harrison Ford took a break from
carpentry to play Han Solo, Reynolds was reportedly

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.000
offered the role.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.130
In the early 1980s, the male lead in Terms
of Endearment was written especially for Reynolds,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.610
but the star said no, as he'd already committed
to Stroker Ace.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.960
The role went to Jack Nicholson, and Reynolds
regrets his decision even today.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.420
Then in 1990, Reynolds reportedly turned down
the starring role in Pretty Woman due to the

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.400
age gap between himself and Julia Roberts.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.840
While these choices didn't affect his career
at the time, they definitely hurt his legacy.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.580
Instead, Reynolds retreated to TV, starring
in sitcoms for much of the late '80s and early

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.700
'90s, appearing as a disembodied voice in
Out of This World and an ex-football star

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.700
in Evening Shade.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.840
He even won his first and only Emmy for his
performance in Evening Shade.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.310
Unfortunately, after four seasons, the show
went off the air and Reynolds went back to

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.000
making bad movies, until 1997.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.520
A thwarted comeback

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.240
In 1997, Reynolds got the comeback role of
a lifetime.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.150
And no, it wasn't in the Mr. Bean movie.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.380
"But I love my country.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.380
I can’t stand the idea of a bunch of Frenchies
owning America’s greatest painting.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:16.680
Let's get on with it."

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.620
Boogie Nights was the film that made Paul
Thomas Anderson a famous director to watch,

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:24.280
and a role in the film gave Reynolds acclaim
that he hadn't seen in a long time.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.610
But despite overwhelming critical praise,
Reynolds didn't really go anywhere after playing

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.280
Jack Horner.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.020
So why didn't Boogie Nights work for Reynolds
like Pulp Fiction did for John Travolta?

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.320
Well, first of all, Reynolds didn't actually
like Boogie Nights.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.230
Reynolds told The Guardian that even though
he's never seen the movie all of the way through,

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:42.510
he still hates it, and that working on it
was a nightmare, mostly citing creative differences

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.510
with Anderson.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.220
Still, Reynolds' rage also extended to the
cast, and according to his book But Enough

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.880
About Me, Reynolds almost got in a fist fight
with co-star Thomas Jane.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.230
Though the shoot was difficult, Anderson had
no complaints about Reynolds's performance.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.180
He even offered him a role in his next film,
Magnolia, but Reynolds turned it down, saying,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.220
"I'd done my picture with Paul Thomas Anderson,
that was enough for me."

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:02.760
Personal problems

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.820
In addition to his spotty film career, things
were also pretty difficult in Reynolds's personal

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:07.320
life.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.300
After marrying actress Loni Anderson in 1988,
the couple divorced in 1993.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.489
It was not amicable, and Reynolds called the
marriage, quote, "a really dumb move" while

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.549
Anderson lobbed accusations of abuse.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:23.250
When the smoke cleared, Reynolds agreed to
pay Anderson a little under $250,000, though

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.420
he didn't complete the transaction right away.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.080
It took 22 years for Reynolds to complete
his payments.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.520
In 1996, the actor filed for bankruptcy, but
was able to keep his large ranch in Florida,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.020
which he eventually sold anyhow.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.760
By 2014, money was still a problem, so he
held an auction of some of his personal belongings,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.470
including props, costumes, and awards from
his films.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:45.540
However, other items he tried to sell seemed
a little more desperate, like his voided credit

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:46.040
card.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:47.580
He's still busy

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:51.100
Despite these bumps in the road, Reynolds
is still working — just not always on the

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:51.700
big screen.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.620
"Burt F------ Reynolds?"

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.480
"who else could keep this town running? "

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.620
In addition to popping up in a few voice roles,
he created the Burt Reynolds Institute, an

00:05:00.631 --> 00:05:04.490
acting studio in Florida where aspiring stars
can learn from Reynolds himself.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:08.790
Even now, at over 80 years old, Reynolds recently
completed the film Dog Years, and has multiple

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.900
films set for release in the near future.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.320
For a guy who's been in the business for six
decades, that's incredibly impressive.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.831
Reynolds' star may not shine as bright today
as it did 40 years ago, but he's still an

00:05:17.831 --> 00:05:20.470
icon who'll always have a place in film history.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.690
And a few bedroom walls.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:22.760
Thanks for watching!

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.810
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.020
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

